  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -12 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$",
  "worked_solution": "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -12 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -12k-24 \\\\ 9k+18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -12k-24=0 \\\\ 9k+18=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-2.$",
